       Attach the specified _f_i_l_e_s_y_s_t_e_m to the jail identified by JID _j_a_i_l_i_d or
       name _j_a_i_l_n_a_m_e.  From now on this file system tree can be managed from
       within a jail if the jjaaiilleedd property has been set.  To use this func‐
       tionality, the jail needs the aallllooww..mmoouunntt and aallllooww..mmoouunntt..zzffss parame‐
       ters set to 11 and the eennffoorrccee__ssttaattffss parameter set to a value lower
       than 22.

       You cannot attach a jailed dataset's children to another jail.  You can
       also not attach the root file system of the jail or any dataset which
       needs to be mounted before the zfs rc script is run inside the jail, as
       it would be attached unmounted until it is mounted from the rc script
       inside the jail.

       To allow management of the dataset from within a jail, the jjaaiilleedd prop‐
       erty has to be set and the jail needs access to the _/_d_e_v_/_z_f_s device.
       The qquuoottaa property cannot be changed from within a jail.

       After a dataset is attached to a jail and the jjaaiilleedd property is set, a
       jailed file system cannot be mounted outside the jail, since the jail
       administrator might have set the mount point to an unacceptable value.

       See jail(8) for more information on managing jails.  Jails are a
       FreeBSD feature and are not relevant on other platforms.

